{"name":"Carmine Carnation","hex":"#ad4b53","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carmine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A purplish-red pigment, made from dye obtained from the cochineal beetle; carminic acid or any of its derivatives.","A purplish-red colour, resembling that pigment."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the purplish red colour shade carmine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carmine"},{"term":"carnation","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of Eurasian plant widely cultivated for its flowers.\noriginally, Dianthus caryophyllus\nother members of genus Dianthus and hybrids","originally, Dianthus caryophyllus"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rosy pink or red colour.","Of a human flesh color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carnation"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carmine","blocks":[{"text":"Carmine – also called cochineal, cochineal extract, crimson lake, or carmine lake – is a pigment of a bright-red color obtained from the aluminium complex derived from carminic acid. Specific code names for the pigment include natural red 4, C.I. 75470, or E120. Carmine is also a general term for a particularly deep-red color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Carmine.svg/330px-Carmine.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmine","wikidata":"Q320617"},{"title":"Dianthus caryophyllus","blocks":[{"text":"Dianthus caryophyllus, commonly known as carnation or clove pink, is a species of Dianthus native to the Mediterranean region. While its exact natural range is uncertain due to extensive cultivation over the last 2,000 years, wild carnations are most common in the Mediterranean region. Carnations are prized for their wide variety of colors, delicate fringed petals, and fragrance, often described as spicy, clove-like, or reminiscent of a combination of cinnamon and nutmeg, hence the common name \"clove pink\". This aroma has made carnations a popular choice for use in perfumes, potpourri, and scented products. Culturally, carnations are associated with affection, distinction, and maternal love, with variations depending on color and area within its wide geographical range."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/W_carnation4051.jpg/330px-W_carnation4051.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dianthus_caryophyllus","wikidata":"Q158984"},{"title":"Carmine (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Carmine color is the general term for some deep red colors that are very slightly purplish but are generally slightly closer to pure red than the color crimson is. Some rubies have the color shown below as rich carmine. The deep dark red color shown at right as carmine is the color of the raw unprocessed pigment, but lighter, richer, or brighter colors are produced when the raw pigment is processed, some of which are shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Carmine.png/330px-Carmine.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carmine_(color)","wikidata":"Q10296772"},{"title":"Shades of pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink colors are usually light or desaturated shades of reds, roses, and magentas which are created on computer and television screens using the RGB color model and in printing with the CMYK color model. As such, it is an arbitrary classification of color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Color_icon_pink_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_pink_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_pink","wikidata":"Q19847189"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Dactylopius_coccus_%28Barlovento%29_04_i